WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.320
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:12.880
As you see, this is not our usual intro.&nbsp;
Why is that? Because today we are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.800
simulate the process of real painting&nbsp;
on a computer, and we’ll find out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.800
all previous techniques mix paint incorrectly,&nbsp;
and finally, and create this beautiful image.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.960
Now, there are plenty of previous&nbsp;
techniques that help us paint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.640
digitally, so, why do we&nbsp;
need a new paper for this?

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:39.360
Well, believe it or not, these previous methods&nbsp;
think differently about the shape that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.200
paint has to take, the more sophisticated&nbsp;
ones even simulate the diffusion of paint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:52.560
which is fantastic. They all do these things a&nbsp;
little differently, but they agree on one thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.600
And, here comes the problem. The one thing&nbsp;
they agree on is that blue plus yellow equals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:07.840
a creamy color. But, wait a second… let’s&nbsp;
actually try this. Many of you know already what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:15.200
is coming…of course. In real life, blue plus&nbsp;
yellow is not a creamy color, it is green.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:23.920
Does the new method know this?&nbsp;
Yes it does! But only this one.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.680
And we can try the similar experiments over and&nbsp;
over and over again. The results are the same.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.600
So, how is this even possible? Does no one&nbsp;
know that blue plus yellow equals green?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.360
Well, of course they know, but a&nbsp;
proper simulation of pigments mixing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:47.680
is very challenging. For instance, it requires&nbsp;
keeping track of pigment concentrations, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.160
we also have to simulate subsurface&nbsp;
scattering, which is the absorption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.800
and scattering of light within a volume of&nbsp;
these pigments. In some critical applications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:03.440
just this part can take several hours to days&nbsp;
to compute for a challenging case. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:10.640
hold on to your papers, because this new technique&nbsp;
can do all this correctly, and in real time. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:16.560
loved this visualization as it is really dense&nbsp;
in information, and super easy to read at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:23.520
same time. That is quite a challenge, and in my&nbsp;
opinion, just this one figure could win an award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:31.040
by itself. As you see, most of the time, it runs&nbsp;
easily with 60, or higher frames per second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:39.440
and even in the craziest cases, it can compute all&nbsp;
this about 30 times per second. That is insanity.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.080
So, what do we get for all this effort? A&nbsp;
more realistic digital painting experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.040
For instance, with the new method, color&nbsp;
mixing now feels absolutely amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:58.480
and if we feel like applying a ton of paint&nbsp;
and let it stain the paper, we get something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:04.400
much more lifelike too. Artists who try this&nbsp;
will appreciate these a great deal I am sure.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:11.040
Especially that the authors also made this paint&nbsp;
color mixing technique available for everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:17.840
free of charge. As we noted, computing this kind&nbsp;
of paint mixing simulation is not easy, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.640
using the final technique is, on the other&nbsp;
hand, extremely easy. As easy as it gets. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:31.040
you feel like coding up a simulation and include&nbsp;
this method in it, this is all you need to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.000
Very few paper implementations are this simple to&nbsp;
use. It conceals all the mathematical difficulties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.280
away from you. Extra point for elegance,&nbsp;
and, huge congratulations to the authors!

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:48.320
Now, after nearly every Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers episode where we showcase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:55.360
such an amazing paper, I get a question saying&nbsp;
something like “okay, but when do I get to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:02.480
or use this in the real world?”. And rightfully&nbsp;
so, that is a good question. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:08.000
this previous GauGAN paper was&nbsp;
published in 2019, and here we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.840
just a bit more than 2 years later, and it&nbsp;
has been transferred into a real product.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:21.600
Some machine learning papers also made it to&nbsp;
Tesla’s self driving cars in one or two years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:28.240
so tech transfer from research to real products&nbsp;
is real. But, is it real with this technique?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.480
Yes it is! So much so that it is already available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:38.960
in an app name Rebelle 5, which offers a&nbsp;
next level digital painting experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:44.240
It even simulates different kinds&nbsp;
of papers and how they absorb paint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:52.240
A paper simulation you say? Yes, here, at Two&nbsp;
Minute Papers, we appreciate that a great deal.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.840
If you use this to paint something, please&nbsp;
make sure to leave a comment or tweet at me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.920
I would love to see your scholarly&nbsp;
paintings! What a time to be alive!

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:45.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

